// content written for these tests in one plain register (short sentences, first person, little
// punctuation beyond commas). TWIN is more of the same register, not in the baseline, and must not be
// flagged. PLANT is the opening of Henry James, The Portrait of a Lady (1881), in the public domain:
// another author's voice among Dustin's, which the design's test table says must be flagged.

export const BASELINE = [
  `The truck quit a mile past the low-water crossing. I let it roll to the shoulder and sat there with the engine ticking. The river was up. You could hear it from the road, a steady sound like wind in a field of maize. I got out and looked at the hood for a while, which did nothing, and then I started walking.

It was hot for April. The cedar along the fence line smelled like pencil shavings. A hawk sat on the wire and watched me go by. I thought about calling Dad, but the phone had one bar and then none. So I walked.

At the gate to the Harlan place there was a new lock. I remembered the old one, a chain wrapped twice and a padlock nobody ever closed. This one was brass and it was shut. Someone had cut the grass along the drive, too. The house sat back in the live oaks the way it always had, the porch sagging at the east corner, the windows dark.

I stood at the gate longer than I meant to. Mrs. Harlan used to give us tea in jelly glasses. She kept a jar of fossils on the windowsill, little coiled shells from the creek bed, and she let us hold them if our hands were dry. I had not thought about that jar in twenty years.

A dog barked somewhere behind the barn. Then a screen door slapped. A man came down the steps and started up the drive toward me, not in a hurry. He wore a feed-store cap and carried nothing in his hands. When he got close enough I could see he was younger than me.

"Help you?" he said.

"My truck died," I said. "Down by the crossing."

He looked past me at the road, as if the truck might be standing there. "River's up," he said.

"I noticed."

He almost smiled. Then he took a key out of his pocket and opened the lock.`,

  `We ate supper at the kitchen table because the dining room was full of boxes. His name was Wade. He had bought the place from the bank in February, he said, and he was still finding things. A saddle in the crawl space. A box of letters in the pantry, tied with baling twine. He had not opened the letters. It did not seem right, he said, and I agreed with him.

He made chili out of a can and cut up an onion to go on top. The onion was the best part. We drank water from the tap, which tasted like iron and limestone, the way it always had out here. I told him about the jelly glasses. He said he had found a whole case of them in the barn.

After supper he walked me out to the porch. The sun was down behind the ridge and the sky over the river had gone the color of a peach. Bats came out of the barn in a long ribbon. We watched them without talking. I could hear the water again, louder now, or maybe the night was just quieter.

"You can sleep on the couch," he said. "Tow truck won't come out till the crossing drops."

"How long is that?"

"Day. Maybe two."

I thought about the job I was supposed to start on Monday. I thought about my apartment in Fort Worth, the plant on the sill I had forgotten to water. None of it felt very close. I said the couch would be fine.

He brought me a quilt that smelled like cedar and mothballs. I lay on the couch in the front room and looked at the ceiling. There was a water stain in the shape of a rabbit. I remembered that stain. I had lain on this same floor as a boy, sick with a fever, and Mrs. Harlan had told me the rabbit was watching over me. I fell asleep before I could decide whether I believed her.`,

  `In the morning the river was still high. Wade was already out at the barn when I woke. I made coffee in a pot I found under the sink and carried two cups out across the yard. The grass was wet and cold on my ankles.

He was working on the tractor. Or he was looking at it, the way I had looked at my truck. The hood was up and there were tools laid out on a feed sack in a neat row. He took the coffee and said thanks and went back to looking.

"Fuel line," he said after a while. "Maybe."

I did not know anything about tractors. I held the flashlight while he worked. It was easy to hold a flashlight. The barn smelled like hay and old oil and something sweet I could not name. Swallows went in and out through a gap under the eaves. Their nests were mud cups stuck to the rafters, and you could see the small heads looking down.

Around ten he got the engine to turn over. It coughed and caught and then ran rough, and blue smoke came out the stack. He let it run. He did not smile, exactly, but he stood up straighter.

"Want to see the creek?" he said.

We walked down past the stock tank. The pasture was full of bluebonnets and the pink ones, the evening primrose, which is open in the morning anyway. The creek ran clear over flat limestone. In a dry year you could walk right down the middle of it. Today it was shin deep and moving fast.

He showed me a place where the rock had a row of dents in it, each one as big as a skillet. I knew what they were. Everybody who grows up around here knows. Still, I crouched down and put my hand in one, and the water pulled at my fingers, and for a second I was nine years old again.`,

  `Wade's sister called at noon. I could hear her voice through the phone from across the room, high and quick. He said yes a few times and then no, and then he walked out onto the porch and shut the door behind him. I washed the cups. There was nothing else to wash.

When he came back in he sat down at the table and put the phone face down. He said his sister thought he was a fool for buying the place. She said the house would fall down around him. She said there was no money in cattle anymore and never would be again.

"Is she right?" I asked.

"Probably." He turned the phone over and turned it back. "About the money, anyway."

I sat down across from him. The table had initials carved into the corner, and I ran my thumb over them. B.H. and R.H. and a heart that someone had tried to scratch out. The Harlans had two sons. I had known the younger one a little. He moved to Oklahoma and sold insurance, I think, or maybe that was someone else.

"I found something," Wade said. "In the letters."

"I thought you didn't open them."

"I didn't. One of them was open already." He got up and went to the pantry and came back with a single envelope. The stamp was a four-cent Lincoln. The paper inside was thin as a moth wing. He did not unfold it. He just set it on the table between us.

"It's got your name on it," he said.

I looked at the envelope for a long time. My name was written in pencil on the back, in handwriting I knew but could not place. Outside, the tractor was still running where he had left it, a low steady sound, like the river.`,
];

export const TWIN = `By afternoon the water had dropped a foot. You could see the mud line on the fence posts where it had been. I walked down to the crossing to look at my truck. Somebody had stuck a note under the wiper blade. It said the county would tow it if it was still there on Friday, and it was signed by a deputy whose name I didn't recognize.

I put the note in my pocket and sat on the tailgate. The sun was warm on my back. A heron stood in the shallows on the far side, very still, and then it wasn't there anymore. I didn't see it go.

I had the letter in my other pocket. I had not read it. Wade had not pushed me, which I liked about him. He just said it was mine now and went back out to the barn. I had carried it around all morning like a stone.

The handwriting was Mrs. Harlan's. I knew that now. It had come to me in the shower, of all places, the way her capital R looped back on itself. She used to write out recipe cards in that hand and tape them inside the cabinet doors.

I took the letter out and held it. The envelope was soft from being handled. I turned it over. My name, in pencil. Under it, very small, a date. The summer I was nine. The summer my mother left and I stayed out here for eight weeks, and nobody told me why.

A truck came down the road from the other side and stopped at the edge of the water. The driver leaned out his window and looked at the crossing. Then he backed up and turned around. I watched him go. I put the letter back in my pocket, and I walked up the hill to the house.`;

export const PLANT = `Under certain circumstances there are few hours in life more agreeable than the hour dedicated to the ceremony known as afternoon tea. There are circumstances in which, whether you partake of the tea or not, some people of course never do, the situation is in itself delightful. Those that I have in mind in beginning to unfold this simple history offered an admirable setting to an innocent pastime. The implements of the little feast had been disposed upon the lawn of an old English country-house, in what I should call the perfect middle of a splendid summer afternoon. Part of the afternoon had waned, but much of it was left, and what was left was of the finest and rarest quality. Real dusk would not arrive for many hours; but the flood of summer light had begun to ebb, the air had grown mellow, the shadows were long upon the smooth, dense turf. They lengthened slowly, however, and the scene expressed that sense of leisure still to come which is perhaps the chief source of one's enjoyment of such a scene at such an hour. From five o'clock to eight is on certain occasions a little eternity; but on such an occasion as this the interval could be only an eternity of pleasure. The persons concerned in it were taking their pleasure quietly, and they were not of the sex which is supposed to furnish the regular votaries of the ceremony I have mentioned.

The shadows on the perfect lawn were straight and angular; they were the shadows of an old man sitting in a deep wicker-chair near the low table on which the tea had been served, and of two younger men strolling to and fro, in desultory talk, in front of him. The old man had his cup in his hand; it was an unusually large cup, of a different pattern from the rest of the set and painted in brilliant colours. He disposed of its contents with much circumspection, holding it for a long time close to his chin, with his face turned to the house. His companions had either finished their tea or were indifferent to their privilege; they smoked cigarettes as they continued to stroll.`;
